<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Feargus O'Connor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1796">1796</a>–<a href="/years/1855">1855</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Feargus Edward O'Connor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Feargus O'Connor was an Irish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartism">Chartist</a> leader, Member of Parliament and advocate of the Land Plan, which sought to provide smallholdings for the labouring classes. Elected MP for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-cork">County Cork</a> in 1832, he founded the Northern Star at Leeds in 1837, and it became the voice of the Chartist movement. In 1845 he founded the Chartist Cooperative Land Company, which was declared illegal in 1851. Elected MP for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottingham">Nottingham</a> in 1847, he organised the Chartist meeting on Kennington Common in 1848. After striking fellow MPs in 1852 he was confined to an asylum; 40,000 people witnessed his funeral procession.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/feargus-oconnor.jpg" alt="Feargus O'Connor" width="330" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Feargus_Edward_O'Connor-crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>O'Connor was born on 18 July 1796 at Connorville house in west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-cork">County Cork</a>, into a prominent Protestant family. His father, Roger O'Connor, and his uncle Arthur O'Connor were active in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/society-of-united-irishmen">United Irishmen</a>, and his elder brother Francis became a general in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>'s army. He studied law at Trinity College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, without taking a degree and was called to the Irish bar about 1820; his father disinherited him for taking the oath of allegiance this required. His first known speech, in 1822 at Enniskeen, denounced landlords and the Protestant clergy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elected for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-cork">County Cork</a> as a Repeal candidate, O'Connor entered Parliament as a follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniel-oconnell">Daniel O'Connell</a> but broke with him over his yielding to the Whigs. Unseated in 1835 for lacking the property qualification, he turned to the working men of the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, denouncing the Poor Law Amendment Act of 1834 and calling for manhood suffrage at huge meetings. The Northern Star, first published on 18 November 1837, soon became the most widely bought provincial newspaper in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and his weekly front-page letter was often read aloud at meetings.</p><p>Leaders of the London Working Men's Association such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-lovett">William Lovett</a> disliked his confrontational style. O'Connor was no insurrectionist but favoured intimidating the authorities through monster meetings and mass petitions. After the petition of 1839 was rejected and the Newport Rising followed, in which he took no part, he was sentenced in May 1840 to 18 months in York Castle.</p><p>His Land Plan, set out in A Practical Work on the Management of Small Farms, settled working families on plots of two to four acres; £112,100 was subscribed, six estates were bought, and the first, Heronsgate, renamed O'Connorsville, opened in May 1847. A House of Commons inquiry reported in June 1848 that the company could not meet its shareholders' expectations and that its books had been imperfectly kept.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Under strain, O'Connor drank heavily, and in 1849 the Commons rejected the Charter by 222 votes to 17. He quarrelled with Ernest Jones, Julian Harney and Thomas Clark, and after the assaults of June 1852 he was kept in Dr Thomas Harrington Tuke's asylum in Chiswick until 1854. He died at Notting Hill Gate on 30 August 1855 and was buried at Kensal Green. Early historians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartism">Chartism</a> blamed his egotism for much of its failure, but Dorothy Thompson called him the movement's most well-loved man, and more recent historians have judged him more favourably.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Feargus O'Connor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ireland. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1834" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Feargus O'Connor"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4993 5724h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4993 5724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1834">What was here in 1834? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
